I built the workflow and saved it as slot w3-B2. It passes the lint check with 0 errors, and the situations check reported no problems. One live chat turn worked: the agent opened as Freya Bank's digital assistant, said the call is recorded and asked "Am I speaking with Ms Lee?". That one turn is the only test I ran; I did not test any calls past the opening.

The workflow starts with the date-facts code node unchanged and runs opening, then identity, then the account lookup with your hook set word for word. After that a router states every late product and hands off to a negotiation node. Each of the six situations has its own node, and a goodbye node ends the call. The two rules in `unhandled_rules` are written word for word into the identity and negotiation nodes. The generator is `out/w3-B2.gen.py`.

Where I went against the extracted situations, because Brief B wins:
- **dispute and already_paid:** the extraction says they close the call with no way back to the main path. Brief B rule 9 says a dispute or "already paid" on one product does not end the call. These nodes now add a note for that product, then go back to negotiation if other products are still open. They close the call only when no products remain.
- **cannot_pay_transfer:** with several products, a flat refusal is counted per product. So the node notes that product as refused and moves on to the next one, instead of closing at once. A request for a plan or lower instalments still transfers to the restructuring queue.
- **Hardship:** when the customer declines support, the node adds a hardship note for each product without a promise, as Brief B requires.

